  "query_preview": "..\n\nHealthy coverage of diurnal cumulus this afternoon wanes in coverage \nthrough the evening as temperatures remain seasonable in the low \n80s. Dry conditions persist overnight as the mid-level ridge axis \nslides east in favor of broad longwave troughing that lingers\nthrough the weekend. This brings a transition to a wetter pattern \nfor the back half of the work week and possibly into the weekend. \n\nThe 12z KDTX RAOB sampled a very dry airmass with PWAT of 0.75\", \nwhich will take a good deal of moisture advection to overcome before \nprecipitation can reach the ground. This process begins over the \nSaginaw Valley late Wednesday morning, starting with an increase in \nhigh cloud followed by increasing PoPs through the afternoon. The \nrain shield stays anchored to a slow-moving cold front that \neventually shears out to further limit its eastward progress \nthrough most of the day Wednesday. Meaningful progression of the \nrain shield into the Detroit Metro region thus holds off until late \nWednesday night as one of several upcoming shortwaves ripples \nthrough the Great Lakes. Sharp delineation in rain chances/cloud \ncover sets up a sharp thermal gradient across the cwa, with upper \n70s highs near Midland to upper 80s in the urban Detroit footprint. \n\nMore widespread rain chances emerge Thursday-Friday as return flow \nmaintains a steady feed of low level theta-e into lower Michigan. \nThis moisture will be repeatedly engaged during the period by \na train of low amplitude perturbations. Ensemble QPF members through \nthe end of the week generally fall around a half inch or less, \nalthough a handful of 1+ inch totals are in the solution space \naround the Tri Cities. A main limiting factor to higher end totals \nis the lack of strong convective component to this event, although \nthere is still some uncertainty here that will depend on boundary \nlayer conditions. Presence of the trough elevates mid level lapse \nrates toward 6 C/km through the end of the week, but otherwise the \ninflux of mid level dry air and modest boundary layer conditions \nstruggle to generate more than ~1000 J/kg MLCAPE at best. This is \nwith highs right around 80 degrees Thursday and slightly warmer \nFriday. SWODY3 (Thursday) places SE Michigan in a General Thunder \nrisk, while the slightly warmer/higher dewpoint environment could \nboost instability for Friday. \n\nUnsettled pattern continues into the weekend with a strong ridge \nover the Four Corners region and an equally strong low over over \nHudson Bay. Main shift this weekend though will be change in \nsteering flow to the northwest, accelerated by confluence over the \ncentral CONUS between these two systems. While details are still \nfuzzy on coverage/timing, precipitation chances linger Saturday-\nSunday.",
